# Every rule here is load-bearing. What was removed, and why: the Go template's
# Windows and plugin patterns (*.exe, *.dll, *.so, *.dylib — this repository
# builds no plugins and no Windows binaries), the coverage patterns (nothing
# generates them), *.test (nothing runs `go test -c`), .env (secrets are Pulumi
# stack config), go.work (no workspace), and three commented-out lines, which
# are rules that do nothing.

# Compiled output. `task build` runs `go build -o bin/ ./...`, so every binary
# lands here and the repository root stays clean.
bin/

# A bare `go build ./...` still names each binary after its main package and
# leaves it in the working directory, where those names collide with the
# tools/, layers/ and infra/ directory names. The pre-commit forbid-binaries
# hook is the actual guarantee — it asks git what is binary rather than
# matching a name — and this list only keeps `git status` honest.
#
# TestGitignore_CoversEveryBinaryName derives it from the tree, because a
# hand-maintained list went stale three times: twice for a new tool, and once
# for infra/cluster, whose binary is called `cluster` and was never listed.
/charts
/cluster
/etcd
/image
/orphans
/secrets
/stack
/talos
/token
/topology
/[0-9][0-9]-*

# Pulumi. The backup files are the CLI's own; .pulumi/ is its local state when
# a DIY backend is used instead of Pulumi Cloud.
Pulumi.*.yaml.bak

# Stack files. They hold one operator's environment — the org and stack their
# layers point at, and the encrypted Hetzner token — so a public repository
# should not ship them. `task cluster:token` and `task platform:init` write
# them locally; Pulumi.yaml, the project file, stays tracked.
Pulumi.*.yaml
.pulumi/

# Pulumi's go language host compiles each layer to a stable path under here, so
# repeated previews skip the link step.
.cache/

# Task. The remote lock is the exception and is committed on purpose: it pins
# the CONTENT behind the `?ref=vX.Y.Z` tag, so a moved tag stops the build
# instead of silently changing it. Refresh deliberately with `task --download`.
.task/*
!.task/remote/
.task/remote/*
!.task/remote/*.checksum

# Cluster credentials, generated on demand by `task cluster:kubeconfig` and
# `cluster:talosconfig`. Both are cluster-admin.
kubeconfig
talosconfig
*.kubeconfig

# etcd snapshots from `task cluster:etcd-snapshot`.
.backups/

# Stack topologies. Kept local: adminCIDRs is the operator's own egress
# address, and this repository is public. cluster.example.yaml is the committed
# template — copy it to cluster.<stack>.yaml.
infra/cluster/cluster.*.yaml
!infra/cluster/cluster.example.yaml

# docs/ is published — the README maps to it. Two things stay internal:
#
#   BACKLOG.md is planning, not documentation.
#   adr/ is a pending decision. The records are design rationale rather than a
#   development diary, so publishing them is probably right, but that is the
#   author's call and not a default.
docs/BACKLOG.md
docs/adr/

# Local-only lefthook overrides. Gates that belong to a working copy rather
# than to the project; lefthook merges the file automatically when present.
lefthook-local.yml
